# Vehicle Detection and Traffic Analysis using YOLOv8

A practical Computer Vision project for detecting vehicles and exploring traffic analysis from road videos using **YOLOv8, Python, and OpenCV**.

## Overview

This project implements a vehicle detection pipeline using a pretrained YOLOv8 model. It processes road traffic videos, detects different vehicle categories, visualizes detection results, and provides a basic heuristic estimation of traffic density.

The project uses a nighttime driving video to explore object detection under challenging lighting conditions.

## Objectives

- Apply deep learning-based object detection to road traffic videos.
- Identify different vehicle categories.
- Process and annotate video frames.
- Explore simple traffic density estimation.
- Measure inference time as an initial performance indicator.
- Investigate ONNX export as a possible model optimization direction.

## Features

- **Vehicle detection:** Detects cars, motorcycles, buses, and trucks.
- **Video processing:** Uses OpenCV to read and process video frames.
- **Visual output:** Generates an annotated video showing detected vehicles.
- **Traffic estimation:** Calculates an illustrative traffic density level based on the number of detected vehicles.
- **Performance measurement:** Records inference timing information.
- **ONNX exploration:** Includes an optional ONNX export and benchmarking workflow.

## Technologies

| Technology | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Python | Main programming language |
| YOLOv8 | Object detection |
| PyTorch | Deep learning framework |
| OpenCV | Video processing and visualization |
| NumPy | Numerical operations |
| Pandas | Results and CSV processing |
| ONNX | Model export and optimization exploration |

## Project Structure

```text
vehicle-detection-traffic-analysis/
│
├── traffic_ai_optimization.py
├── Cars_driving_at_night.webm
├── README.md
├── requirements.txt
│
└── outputs/
    ├── annotated_traffic.mp4
    ├── traffic_results.csv
    └── traffic_benchmark_frame.jpg
```

*Note: Generated output filenames depend on the script configuration. The input video is not necessarily included in the repository.*## 


The script processes the video, detects vehicles, and generates output files according to its configuration.

## Vehicle Categories

The project focuses on the following COCO vehicle classes:

| Vehicle | COCO Class ID |
|---|---:|
| Car | 2 |
| Motorcycle | 3 |
| Bus | 5 |
| Truck | 7 |

## Traffic Density Estimation

The project uses a simple heuristic based on the number of detected vehicles in a frame.

This provides an initial way to explore traffic conditions. The thresholds are illustrative and should be calibrated using representative traffic datasets before any real-world application.

## Performance Evaluation

Inference-time measurements are collected to explore the computational behavior of the detection pipeline.

An optional ONNX export and benchmarking workflow is also included as an initial optimization experiment.

**Important:** These measurements are preliminary. A rigorous comparison between PyTorch and ONNX requires controlled hardware, consistent input dimensions, warm-up iterations, and repeated measurements.

## Limitations and Future Improvements

This project is an educational prototype and has several possible improvements:

- Calibrate traffic density thresholds using real traffic data.
- Evaluate detection precision and recall on an annotated dataset.
- Compare YOLO model variants.
- Investigate model export and inference with ONNX Runtime.
- Explore quantization and model optimization.
- Measure latency and memory consumption under controlled conditions.
- Investigate deployment on embedded and edge computing platforms.

